window.__hvBdRegister("the-history-of-animals", {"key":"the-history-of-animals","sections":[{"i":0,"j":38,"loc":"Book I (1 of 4)","title":"How animals differ in parts and lives","summary":"Aristotle opens by distinguishing simple parts, which divide into parts like themselves (flesh into flesh), from composite parts such as the hand or face. Parts in different animals may be identical in form, differ by excess or defect within a genus, or correspond only by analogy, as scale to feather. He then surveys differences in modes of life: aquatic and terrestrial animals, stationary and moving ones, gregarious, solitary and social kinds, and differences of diet, dwelling, tameness, voice and temperament. The survey ends by noting that man alone can deliberate and recall the past at will.","ideas":["Parts may be the same in form, differ by more and less, or correspond only by analogy","Animals differ in their lives, actions, habits and parts as well as in body","Social animals share a common object; some, like cranes and bees, follow a ruler","Character traits vary by kind, but only man deliberates"],"terms":[{"t":"homoiomerous (uniform) parts","d":"Parts that divide into portions of the same kind as the whole, such as flesh, bone and blood."},{"t":"genos (genus)","d":"A wide group such as Bird or Fish, within which species differ by excess or defect."},{"t":"analogy","d":"Correspondence of function between parts that are not alike in form, as fish-bone to bone or hand to claw."}]},{"i":39,"j":70,"loc":"Book I (2 of 4)","title":"Common organs and the main animal groups","summary":"Aristotle lists the organs nearly all animals share: mouth and belly, outlets for dry and wet residue, and in many the organs of generation, with touch the one sense common to all. He divides animals into blooded and bloodless, and into viviparous, oviparous and grub-bearing, and explains how an egg differs from a grub. He then treats feet, fins and wings, the points of motion in moving animals, and the great genera: birds, fishes, cetaceans, and the bloodless shelled, soft-shelled, mollusc and insect kinds. He calls all this a preliminary sketch, to be followed by detailed study and then by an account of causes.","ideas":["Touch is the only sense shared by every animal","Blooded versus bloodless and viviparous versus oviparous divide the animal world","All moving blooded animals move with four points of motion","Facts must be gathered first; causes are to be discussed afterwards"],"terms":[{"t":"vivipara and ovipara","d":"Animals that bring forth live young and those that lay eggs; some, like sharks, hatch eggs internally."},{"t":"grub (skōlēx)","d":"A germ from which the whole animal develops entire, unlike an egg, part of which serves as food."}]},{"i":71,"j":111,"loc":"Book I (3 of 4)","title":"The external parts of the human body","summary":"Because man is the animal best known to us, Aristotle begins the detailed account with human parts, as a nation reckons by its own currency. He names the head and skull, the face, eyes, ears, nose, jaws, mouth and tongue, then the neck, trunk, genitals, arms and legs, adding notes on other animals, such as the mole's hidden eyes and the elephant's trunk. Along the way he records signs of character drawn from foreheads, eyebrows, eyes, ears and feet. He ends by noting that in man up and down accord with the universe, and that man excels in touch and taste but is surpassed in other senses.","ideas":["Man is the familiar standard by which other animals are compared","Features of the face are read as signs of character","In man alone the head points upward in relation to the universe","Human touch is the most refined of any animal"],"terms":[{"t":"bregma (sinciput)","d":"The front of the skull, the last bone in the body to become solid after birth."},{"t":"physiognomic signs","d":"Bodily features that the text links to temperament, such as eye colour or the set of the ears."}]},{"i":112,"j":136,"loc":"Book I (4 of 4)","title":"The internal organs of man","summary":"Since human interior parts are largely unknown, Aristotle says they must be studied in animals that resemble man. He describes the brain and its membranes, the ducts from the eye, the windpipe and epiglottis, the lungs, oesophagus, stomach, bowel and mesentery. He gives the heart three cavities, connected with the lung, the great vein and the aorta, and rejects the view that the lung is empty of blood. He then places the midriff, liver, spleen, kidneys and bladder, notes how gall-bladders figure in augury, and refers the reader to his Anatomy for the womb.","ideas":["Inner human anatomy must be inferred from similar animals","The heart has three cavities and alone among organs holds blood in itself","The lung is full of blood in life; dissection misleads observers","Unusual gall-bladders frighten sacrificers who take them as omens"],"terms":[{"t":"great vein and aorta","d":"The two main blood vessels attached to the heart, to which the liver, spleen and kidneys are connected."},{"t":"omentum (caul)","d":"A fatty membrane attached to the middle of the stomach."}]},{"i":137,"j":162,"loc":"Book II (1 of 4)","title":"Limbs, hair and horns of quadrupeds","summary":"Book II turns to parts that differ across genera, starting with viviparous quadrupeds. Aristotle compares their forelegs to hands, describes the elephant's trunk and its way of lying down to sleep, and sets out how limbs bend in quadrupeds, birds and man. He covers diagonal gait, the seal as an imperfect quadruped, the distribution of hair, manes, the camel's hump and foot, and cloven, many-toed and solid hooves. He ends with the huckle-bone and horns, noting that the deer alone has solid antlers and sheds them each year.","ideas":["Quadrupeds bend fore and hind limbs in opposite directions to man's","Man is hairier on the head and front, quadrupeds on the back","No solid-hooved animal has two horns; the Indian ass has one","The deer alone sheds and regrows its solid horns"],"terms":[{"t":"astragalus (huckle-bone)","d":"An ankle bone found in the hind legs of many cloven-footed animals, set upright in the joint."},{"t":"amble","d":"The gait of the lion and the camel, in which the left foot never passes the right."}]},{"i":163,"j":186,"loc":"Book II (2 of 4)","title":"Breasts, genitals, teeth, and the apes","summary":"Aristotle compares the number and position of breasts and teats, the genitals of males and females, and the proportions of upper and lower body in man and other animals as they grow. He sets out differences in teeth: animals with teeth in both jaws or not, tusked and saw-toothed kinds, the shedding of teeth, and the elephant's tusks. He reports, attributing it to Ctesias, the man-eating Indian martichoras with three rows of teeth. He closes with the hippopotamus and the apes, monkeys and baboons, which share features of man and the quadrupeds.","ideas":["Placement and number of breasts vary widely among mammals","Man is born with a larger upper body and grows to the reverse","No animal has both tusks and horns","The ape combines human and quadruped traits, with hand-like feet"],"terms":[{"t":"saw-toothed (karcharodont)","d":"Having sharp teeth that interlock between those of the opposite jaw, as the lion and dog."},{"t":"martichoras","d":"An Indian beast described by Ctesias, which Aristotle reports with the words 'if we are to believe' him."}]},{"i":187,"j":210,"loc":"Book II (3 of 4)","title":"External parts of reptiles, birds and fishes","summary":"Aristotle describes oviparous blooded quadrupeds: they have ear passages but no ears, no breasts, internal testicles and scaly plates. He gives detailed accounts of the crocodile and the chameleon, including its colour changes and long survival after being cut open. Birds are treated next: two backward-bending legs, wings, beaks, feathers, toes, and the wryneck's tongue. He then describes fishes, with gills, fins, scales and eyes but no ears or nostrils, the dolphin's milk, and closes with serpents, sea-centipedes and the echeneis.","ideas":["Oviparous quadrupeds lack ears, breasts and external testicles","The chameleon changes colour when inflated with air","Some broad-tongued birds come nearest to man in articulate sound","Fishes differ chiefly in the number and form of their gills"],"terms":[{"t":"selachians","d":"Cartilaginous fishes such as sharks and rays, which have uncovered gills and are mostly viviparous."},{"t":"echeneis ('ship-holder')","d":"A small rock fish used by some as a charm in lawsuits and love, which only seems to have feet."}]},{"i":211,"j":236,"loc":"Book II (4 of 4)","title":"Internal organs of the blooded animals","summary":"Aristotle now compares the internal organs of blooded animals: windpipe, oesophagus, lung, heart, midriff, liver, spleen and gall-bladder. He notes exceptions, such as a bone in the hearts of some oxen and horses, deer lacking a gall-bladder, and maggots in stags' heads. The four-chambered stomach of horned ruminants is described part by part, against the single stomach of animals with teeth in both jaws. He then covers serpents, with their forked tongues and thirty ribs, and the stomachs, caeca and crops of fishes and birds.","ideas":["Fish have no lung, and most have no oesophagus","Ruminants have paunch, reticulum, echinus and abomasum","Serpents are like saurians lengthened and without legs","Birds vary in crop, oesophagus and caeca"],"terms":[{"t":"ruminant stomach","d":"The four chambers (paunch, reticulum, echinus, enystrum) of horned animals lacking upper front teeth."},{"t":"ambidental","d":"Having front teeth in both jaws, as man, the pig and the dog, which have a single stomach."},{"t":"caeca","d":"Blind appendages of the gut, high up near the stomach in fishes and low down in birds."}]},{"i":237,"j":256,"loc":"Book III (1 of 5)","title":"The organs of generation","summary":"Book III begins with the generative organs, internal in all females and varied in males. Aristotle sets out which animals lack testicles (fishes, serpents) or have them inside, as birds and the dolphin do, and describes the seminal ducts, referring to a lettered diagram. He notes that birds' testicles swell in the breeding season and that a castrated bull has impregnated a cow. He then compares wombs: two-horned in vivipara, near the midriff in birds, and intermediate in sharks and the viper, which are oviparous inside and viviparous outside, and ends with the cotyledons of some pregnant animals.","ideas":["Fishes and serpents have seminal ducts instead of testicles","Bird testicles become large only in the breeding season","The viper's womb resembles that of the selachians","Womb position differs between vivipara and ovipara"],"terms":[{"t":"delphys / hystera (womb)","d":"The body of the womb, from which Aristotle derives the word adelphos (brother)."},{"t":"cotyledons","d":"Attachments in the womb of pregnant horned animals and a few others, to which the embryo is joined."}]},{"i":257,"j":285,"loc":"Book III (2 of 5)","title":"Earlier accounts of the veins and Aristotle's","summary":"Turning to the uniform parts, Aristotle starts with blood and veins, which he thinks primary and poorly treated by earlier writers because they are hard to observe. He quotes the accounts of Syennesis of Cyprus, Diogenes of Apollonia and Polybus, and rejects the common view that the veins begin in the head. He advises studying animals starved thin and then suddenly strangled. His own account traces the great vein and aorta from the heart through the lung, neck, head, arms, liver, spleen, kidneys and legs, citing a Homeric line on the vein along the back.","ideas":["Veins are hard to study because dead vessels collapse","The heart, not the head, is the source of the blood vessels","The great vein passes through the heart like a river widening into a lake","Bleeding at certain veins is linked to pains in particular organs"],"terms":[{"t":"phleps (vein)","d":"The word used for blood vessels generally, applied both to the great vein and to the aorta."},{"t":"aorta","d":"The smaller, sinewy main vessel from the middle chamber of the heart, said to be named because part of it looks full of air."}]},{"i":286,"j":301,"loc":"Book III (3 of 5)","title":"Sinews, fibres, bones and gristle","summary":"Aristotle notes that his account of the veins is easiest to confirm in large, full-blooded animals. He then describes sinews, which he traces to the heart but which are not continuous like the veins, which he compares to a sketch of a human figure. Fibres are placed between sinew and vein, and fibre in the blood is said to make it clot, so that deer's blood hardly coagulates. Bones all connect to the backbone; he covers the skull, jaws, ribs and limbs, the lion's hard bones, fish-spine in fishes and serpents, and gristle.","ideas":["The veins trace the outline of the whole body","Sinews gather at joints and do not grow back if cut","Fibre in the blood causes clotting","Every bone connects to the spine; none stands alone"],"terms":[{"t":"neura (sinews)","d":"Tough cords at the joints and in the heart, fissile lengthwise; the word covers tendons and ligaments."},{"t":"ines (fibres)","d":"Tissue between sinew and vein; a kind found in blood makes it coagulate."}]},{"i":302,"j":327,"loc":"Book III (4 of 5)","title":"Horns, nails, hair, skin and membranes","summary":"Aristotle describes nails, hooves, claws, horns and beaks, which bend and split unlike bone, and whose colour follows the skin. He treats hair at length: its thickness, its greying (which he argues is not a withering), baldness tied to sexual maturity, the effects of castration, and beards on women seen as portents. He reports changes of colour in birds from cold and in sheep from drinking particular rivers, including the Scamander. He ends with skin, membranes, the omentum and the bladder, which do not grow together when cut.","ideas":["Horns, hooves and nails match the colour of the skin; teeth follow the bones","Grey hair is often white from the start, so it is not decay","No boy, woman or eunuch goes bald","Drinking water is said to change the colour of offspring"],"terms":[{"t":"later growths of hair","d":"Hair on the pubes, armpits and chin, appearing only at maturity and only in man."},{"t":"membrane (hymēn)","d":"A thin close skin around bones and viscera that neither splits nor stretches."}]},{"i":328,"j":357,"loc":"Book III (5 of 5)","title":"Flesh, fat, blood, marrow, milk and sperm","summary":"Aristotle describes flesh, the difference between fat and suet, and where animals store fat, as round the kidneys of sheep. He calls blood the most universal and necessary part of blooded animals, describes its colour, taste and clotting, and contrasts its quality by species, sex and age. Marrow follows, then milk, with its whey and curd, how cheese and rennet are made, the large cattle of Epirus, and fodder that increases or stops the flow. The book ends with sperm, correcting Herodotus on Aethiopian sperm and dismissing Ctesias on the elephant's.","ideas":["Fat does not congeal while suet does","Blood is formed from ichor by concoction, and fat from blood","Milk of non-ambidentals curdles and makes cheese","Sperm is always white"],"terms":[{"t":"ichor","d":"Thin, unconcocted blood, abundant in very young animals."},{"t":"pepsis (concoction)","d":"The working-up of bodily fluids by natural heat, by which blood and fat are formed."},{"t":"rennet","d":"Milk found curdled in the stomach of suckling animals, used to curdle milk."}]},{"i":358,"j":376,"loc":"Book IV (1 of 5)","title":"The bloodless genera and the molluscs","summary":"Book IV moves to bloodless animals, which Aristotle divides into four genera: molluscs, soft-shelled crustaceans, shelled testaceans, and insects. He describes the molluscs (octopus, sepia and calamaries): their eight suckered feet, the two long tentacles of some, the tube for expelling water and ink, mouth, crop, stomach and the ink-sac. He notes the internal sepium or pen, the differences between the sexes, and the octopus's arm used in mating. He closes with the kinds of octopus, including the nautilus that lives in a shell.","ideas":["Molluscs have their soft part outside and any hard part inside","Cuttlefish anchor themselves by their tentacles like ships","All molluscs discharge ink when frightened","Some octopus-like animals live in shells"],"terms":[{"t":"malakia (molluscs)","d":"Bloodless animals with flesh outside and any hard part inside, as the cuttle-fish and octopus."},{"t":"mytis","d":"An organ of bloodless animals that takes the place of viscera, bearing the ink-sac in molluscs."}]},{"i":377,"j":394,"loc":"Book IV (2 of 5)","title":"Crustaceans: crawfish, lobster, carid and crab","summary":"Aristotle surveys the crustaceans: crawfish, lobster, the carids or prawns and squillae, and the many kinds of crab, including Phoenician 'horsemen' crabs too fast to catch. He counts their feet, claws, tail-fins and antennae, and gives the male and female differences in the crawfish. A long description of the lobster notes that its right and left claws always differ in size and teeth. He then covers their internal parts, how they take in and expel water, and the male and female organs of the carid and crab.","ideas":["Crustaceans have their hard shell outside and flesh inside","The lobster always has one claw larger than the other","All crustaceans have two teeth and a fleshy tongue-like part","Bloodless animals share with blooded ones only stomach, oesophagus and gut"],"terms":[{"t":"malakostraka (crustaceans)","d":"Animals with a hard outer covering that crushes rather than shatters, such as crabs and crawfish."},{"t":"carid","d":"The prawn and shrimp group, including the hunch-backed kind and the squilla."}]},{"i":395,"j":412,"loc":"Book IV (3 of 5)","title":"Testaceans, urchins, ascidians and sea-nettles","summary":"Aristotle treats the testaceans, whose shell shatters rather than crushes: spiral stromboids, univalves like the limpet, and bivalves that open or are fixed. He describes their shells and inner parts, including the boring proboscis of the purple murex, the gut and the 'poppy'. The hermit crab is placed between crustaceans and testaceans. He then describes sea-urchins, with five teeth and five 'eggs' and a mouth below; the ascidian, wholly enclosed in a leathery shell; and the sea-nettle, which seizes prey and, lacking excretion, resembles a plant.","ideas":["Testaceans differ widely in shell, but less in inner structure","The hermit crab borrows shells and shares in two kinds","The urchin's mouth is below because its food lies below","The sea-nettle is like a plant in having no excretion"],"terms":[{"t":"ostrakoderma (testaceans)","d":"Animals with flesh inside a hard shell that can be shattered, such as snails and oysters."},{"t":"mecon ('poppy')","d":"An organ in testaceans that Aristotle says is a kind of excretion enclosed in a membrane."},{"t":"tethyum (ascidian)","d":"A rock-attached animal enclosed entirely in a substance between hide and shell."}]},{"i":413,"j":433,"loc":"Book IV (4 of 5)","title":"Insects, and the five senses in animals","summary":"Aristotle describes insects: head, middle and trunk, survival when cut in two, the tongue-like organ used to sting or suck, stings, wings and antennae, and a simple gut, with the cicada living on dew. He records strange sea-creatures reported by fishermen. He then sets out the five senses and argues that blooded animals have all of them, though the mole's eyes are stunted under the skin. Fishermen's practice proves that fishes and dolphins hear and smell without visible organs, and that insects, molluscs and testaceans smell and taste.","ideas":["Insect flesh is between shell and flesh and protects itself","There are five senses and touch is common to all","Fishes flee noise and choose bait by smell","Evidence of sight and hearing in testaceans is uncertain"],"terms":[{"t":"entoma (insects)","d":"Animals with nicks on belly or back, whose bodies are hard throughout, between bone and flesh."},{"t":"aisthēsis (sense)","d":"Sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch; Aristotle knows of no other."}]},{"i":434,"j":453,"loc":"Book IV (5 of 5)","title":"Voice, sleep, and the difference of sex","summary":"Aristotle distinguishes sound, voice and language: voice needs a lung and pharynx, and language is voice articulated by tongue and lips. Insects and fishes therefore make only sounds; oviparous quadrupeds have weak voices; birds sing, and young birds can learn songs, so dialects differ by place, while true language belongs to man. He argues from fishermen's evidence that fishes, molluscs and insects sleep, and that man dreams most. Finally he discusses which animals have two sexes, denies that eels have sex, and compares male and female in size, strength and voice.","ideas":["Voice, sound and articulate language are distinct","A bird's song can be learned and improved, unlike mere voice","Fishes sleep, shown by stillness and capture at night","Males are generally stronger in front, females in the hinder parts"],"terms":[{"t":"phōnē (voice)","d":"Sound produced by the pharynx, possible only for animals with a lung."},{"t":"dialektos (language)","d":"Articulation of voice by the tongue, which differs by place and in full form belongs only to man."}]},{"i":454,"j":478,"loc":"Book V (1 of 7)","title":"Plan of the work on generation; modes of copulation","summary":"Aristotle turns from the parts, senses and sexes of animals to their modes of propagation, taking the groups in order and leaving man to the end. He notes that, as with plants, some animals come from parents of their kind and others arise spontaneously from putrefying matter or from secretions inside animals, and that spontaneously generated creatures that copulate produce only imperfect offspring such as nits and grubs. He then describes how each group copulates: quadrupeds, birds, camels, elephants and seals; tortoises and serpents; cartilaginous fishes and cetaceans; molluscs joining by their tentacles; crustaceans, crabs, insects and web-spinning spiders. He rejects the belief that female fishes conceive by swallowing milt, explaining it from similar behaviour in other animals.","ideas":["Animals, like plants, arise either from kindred parents or by spontaneous generation from mud, decay or bodily secretions.","Spontaneously generated creatures that pair produce something imperfect, never the parent kind.","The posture of copulation varies widely by group, from rearward mounting to intertwining and side-by-side union.","In insects the female inserts her organ into the male, the reverse of other animals."],"terms":[{"t":"spontaneous generation","d":"Coming into being from putrefying earth, plants or secretions rather than from parents of the same kind."},{"t":"opisthuretic","d":"Of animals that urinate backwards and so copulate with a rearward presentment, like the lion and hare."}]},{"i":479,"j":500,"loc":"Book V (2 of 7)","title":"Breeding seasons of animals, birds and fishes","summary":"Aristotle sets out when animals pair and breed. Most do so in spring, as winter turns to summer, though man and well-fed domestic animals breed at all seasons, and many time pairing so the young are reared at a good season. He describes the halcyon breeding at the winter solstice, explaining the 'halcyon days' with verses of Simonides, and gives the seasons and number of broods for many fishes, molluscs, testaceans and birds. He stresses that locality strongly affects how often and how well a species breeds. The section closes with remarks on the breeding of domestic birds and the kinds of pigeon.","ideas":["Spring is the general pairing season, but shelter and food let man and domestic animals breed all year.","The halcyon nests at the winter solstice, giving rise to the calm 'halcyon days'.","Most fishes spawn once a year, some two or three times, and locality alters frequency and vigour.","Pigeon, rock-pigeon, ring-dove, stock-dove and turtle-dove are distinguished by size and habits."],"terms":[{"t":"halcyon days","d":"The seven calm days before and after the winter solstice, when the halcyon is said to build its nest and hatch."},{"t":"aphareus","d":"The fin beneath the belly that marks the female tunny."}]},{"i":501,"j":516,"loc":"Book V (3 of 7)","title":"Age of sexual maturity and of generative decline","summary":"Aristotle examines the ages at which animals become sexually capable and when that capacity ends. The first semen is infertile or produces small, weak offspring; in man puberty shows in the voice, sexual organs, breasts and pubic hair, with seed at about fourteen and fertility at about twenty-one. He discusses changes of voice across sex and age, noting that in cattle the cow is deeper than the bull and that gelded males take on female traits. He then lists ages of maturity, gestation periods and breeding lifespans for sheep, goats, swine, dogs, horses, asses, man, camels and elephants, adding practical lore from breeders.","ideas":["Seminal secretion comes before fertility, and the earliest offspring are poorer.","Male voices are generally deeper, though the cow is deeper than the bull.","Each species has a characteristic age of maturity and span of fertility, from swine at eight months to the elephant at ten or more years.","Feeding and condition affect breeding, as with over-fat he-goats and well-fed boars."],"terms":[]},{"i":517,"j":550,"loc":"Book V (4 of 7)","title":"Generation of testaceans, sponges, crustaceans and molluscs","summary":"Aristotle turns to generation proper, beginning with testaceans, which do not copulate. The purple murex and ceryx deposit a 'honeycomb', but he holds that they and other shell-fish grow spontaneously out of mud and decaying matter, and he describes the murex's purple 'bloom' and how fishermen extract it. He treats the pinna and its pinna-guard, the hermit-crab, sea-anemones, the star-fish and three kinds of sponge, discussing whether the sponge has sensation. He then describes how female crawfish carry their eggs under the tail, where crawfish and lobsters live, and how the octopus, sepia and calamary lay, guard and hatch their spawn, with the young sepia growing from a white substance like a bird's yolk.","ideas":["Testaceans are generated spontaneously in mud, slime or sand according to kind, not by copulation.","The murex's 'bloom', between the mecon and neck, is the source of purple dye.","Sponges are attached growths fed by slime; some say they contract when touched, which others doubt.","Crustaceans brood their eggs beneath their bodies; cephalopods hatch them where they are laid."],"terms":[{"t":"testaceans","d":"Shelled animals such as the murex, oyster and scallop, which Aristotle treats as non-copulating."},{"t":"bloom (anthos)","d":"The organ in the purple murex yielding the purple colour."}]},{"i":551,"j":570,"loc":"Book V (5 of 7)","title":"Generation and metamorphosis of insects","summary":"Aristotle explains that insects produce grubs, from which the whole adult forms, unlike young that grow from part of an egg. Some insects come from parents of their kind, while others arise spontaneously from dew, mud, dung, timber, hair, flesh or excrement, including three kinds of intestinal worm. He traces the stages from caterpillar to chrysalis to butterfly and describes the large horned grub whose cocoons women unwind to weave a fabric invented by Pamphila of Cos. He also describes gnats arising from ascarids in slime, flies from dung, grubs in snow, a winged creature in Cyprian copper furnaces, and the one-day ephemeron. He gives the time of insect development and says insects die from shrivelling of their organs.","ideas":["Insect grubs change entire into the adult, passing through a quiescent stage without feeding.","Many insects are generated spontaneously, with the sun's heat or wind as the primary cause.","The silk-like fabric from cocoons is credited to Pamphila of Cos.","Life is found even in snow and fire, as the salamander story is said to show."],"terms":[{"t":"chrysalis","d":"The quiescent, hard-shelled stage between caterpillar and butterfly, in which the creature neither feeds nor voids excrement."},{"t":"nympha","d":"The defined, motionless stage of the bee or wasp larva sealed in its cell."},{"t":"ephemeron","d":"An insect on the river Hypanis said to live only one day."}]},{"i":571,"j":588,"loc":"Book V (6 of 7)","title":"Bees, their rulers, honey, and wasps","summary":"Aristotle reviews competing accounts of how bees are generated: that they fetch their young from flowers, that the rulers beget the working bees, or that drones are male and bees female. He describes the two kinds of ruler, their separate cells below the comb, the kinds of bee and the stingless drone, and why a hive fails with too few or too many kings. He holds that wax comes from flowers and tree-gum while honey falls from the atmosphere and is only gathered, giving reasons for this view. He then describes the development of grubs in the cells, the lifespan of bees, unusual bees and honeys of Pontus, and the hexagonal combs of anthrenae and wasps.","ideas":["Several theories of bee generation are recorded without one being fully settled.","Ruler-bees are larger, grow in separate cells, and have stings they never use.","Honey is said to be distilled from dew and the air, not made from flowers.","Anthrenae and wasps build hexagonal combs of fibre, not wax."],"terms":[{"t":"ruler (king) bee","d":"The large bee, called by some the 'mother', bred in special cells and thought to contribute to the hive's generation."},{"t":"drone","d":"The largest bee, stingless and inactive."}]},{"i":589,"j":617,"loc":"Book V (7 of 7)","title":"Spiders, locusts, cicadas, lice; oviparous quadrupeds and serpents","summary":"Aristotle describes the breeding of ants, scorpions and spiders, noting that young phalangia and scorpions often kill the mother. He explains how grasshoppers and locusts lay eggs in the ground and die, and gives a long account of the two kinds of cicada, where they live, how the nymph becomes the singing male, and their habits. He treats lice, fleas and bugs, which produce nits that generate nothing, the louse-disease said to have killed Alcman and Pherecydes, sea-lice, clothes-moths, mites, the 'faggot-bearer' grub and the fig-wasp used in caprification. He ends with tortoises, lizards, crocodiles and serpents, explaining that the viper is outwardly viviparous after being oviparous within.","ideas":["Young spiders and scorpions may kill their parent after hatching.","Only the male cicada sings; the nymph is best eating before its husk breaks.","Lice, fleas and bugs arise from flesh, moisture or decay and produce sterile nits.","The crocodile grows from an egg no larger than a goose's to twenty-six feet."],"terms":[{"t":"tettigometra","d":"The cicada nymph that grows in the ground before turning into the adult."},{"t":"psen (fig-wasp)","d":"The insect from the wild fig that enters the cultivated fig and keeps the fruit from dropping."}]},{"i":618,"j":633,"loc":"Book VI (1 of 9)","title":"Birds' eggs and the chick's development","summary":"Aristotle opens Book VI with birds: how often different kinds lay, how many eggs they lay, and where they nest. He describes the two-coloured egg, how shape is thought to indicate sex, and hatching by dung-heaps in Egypt. He traces how the egg forms after copulation and argues against the view that wind-eggs are leftovers from earlier matings, explaining how they can become fertile if the hen is trodden again. He contrasts yolk and white and then follows the chick's development day by day: the heart as a beating speck of blood by the third day, the large eyes, the two navel-strings to yolk and outer membrane, and the arrangement of membranes and liquids.","ideas":["Domestic birds lay throughout the year; birds of prey are unprolific, except the kestrel.","Wind-eggs are formed without copulation and can turn fertile if the hen is trodden early enough.","The heart appears first as a pulsing blood-speck in the white.","The chick lives from the white, while the yolk nourishes it through a navel-string."],"terms":[{"t":"wind-egg","d":"An egg produced without copulation, which never hatches; also called zephyr-egg."},{"t":"navel-string","d":"The cord through which the embryo draws nourishment, here from the yolk."}]},{"i":634,"j":651,"loc":"Book VI (2 of 9)","title":"Hatching, pigeons, birds of prey, cuckoo and peafowl","summary":"Aristotle completes the chick's development to hatching, when the yolk is drawn into its body. He discusses infertile and twin eggs and the breeding of pigeons in detail: pairing with a kiss, alternate brooding by day and night, and many broods a year. He reports Herodorus' claim that vultures come from an unknown land, which he rejects, and the eagle's habit of laying three eggs and rearing fewer, citing verses ascribed to Musaeus. He weighs the belief that the cuckoo is a transformed hawk, noting how the two differ, and describes its laying in other birds' nests. The section ends with brooding habits of geese, crows, ring-doves and partridges, and the breeding of peafowl.","ideas":["Hen-pigeons kiss before pairing and may tread one another, producing wind-eggs.","Birds of prey commonly drive their young from the nest; the crow keeps feeding hers.","The cuckoo resembles the hawk only in colour and lays its eggs in other birds' nests.","Peafowl eggs are set under hens because the peacock attacks the brooding peahen."],"terms":[]},{"i":652,"j":674,"loc":"Book VI (3 of 9)","title":"Cartilaginous fishes, cetaceans and the seal","summary":"Aristotle explains that cartilaginous fishes are first oviparous within and then viviparous, apart from the fishing-frog, and compares their wombs and eggs with those of birds. He describes embryo development in fishes, the egg-cases of dog-fish and skate, and the smooth shark, whose embryo hangs from the womb by a navel-string like a quadruped's. He notes that these fishes carry eggs and embryos at once, that some can take their young back inside, and that the rhinobatus seems to be a cross of angel-fish and skate. He then shows that dolphins, whales and porpoises bear live young, breathe with lungs and suckle, and describes the seal as an amphibious viviparous animal that bears and suckles on land.","ideas":["Cartilaginous fishes hatch eggs internally and then bear live young.","The smooth shark's embryo is attached by a navel-string to the womb.","Cetaceans have blow-holes, lungs and milk, and bear one or two young.","Fishermen nick dolphins' tails to learn how long they live."],"terms":[{"t":"selachians (cartilaginous fishes)","d":"Sharks, rays and their kin, with gristly skeletons and internal development of the young."},{"t":"rhinobatus","d":"A fish with a skate's front and an angel-fish's rear, taken as evidence of crossing between kinds."}]},{"i":675,"j":693,"loc":"Book VI (4 of 9)","title":"Spawning of bony fishes and spontaneous fry","summary":"Aristotle describes the bifurcate womb and granular roe of oviparous fishes and notes that the erythrinus and channa are always caught pregnant. Only eggs sprinkled with milt by the male become young fish; he notes that the same happens in the cuttle-fish. He describes inshore spawning, the pipe-fish that splits to release its eggs, and early development of the embryo, rejecting the view that young fishes form like grubs. He then treats river and marsh fishes, especially the sheat-fish, whose male guards the spawn for forty or fifty days, and the carp. Finally he argues from the pond near Cnidos and the 'froth' fry of Attica that some fishes arise spontaneously from mud and sand.","ideas":["Eggs become fertile only when the male sheds milt over them after spawning.","The fish embryo forms at the upper end of the egg with the eyes first, unlike a grub.","The male sheat-fish guards the spawn against small fishes.","Some mullet and the 'froth' fry are generated from mud and sand, not from eggs."],"terms":[{"t":"milt","d":"The male fish's seminal fluid, shed over the laid eggs."},{"t":"aphros (froth)","d":"Small fry said to arise spontaneously from sandy ground and foam."}]},{"i":694,"j":708,"loc":"Book VI (5 of 9)","title":"Eels and the seasons of fish spawning","summary":"Aristotle argues that eels are produced neither by pairing nor from eggs, because no eel has milt or spawn and eels reappear in drained ponds after rain. He rejects the view that worms found in eels are their young, saying eels come from the 'earth's guts' that grow in mud. He then gives general rules on fish gestation, which lasts a number of days divisible by seven, and on fish suffering during pregnancy. He lists the order in which shoal fishes spawn, notes the prolific sprat and fishing-frog, the pipe-fish and the tunny, and stresses that seasons vary with locality. He closes the topic with the conger's hidden spawn, and announces viviparous land animals and man as next.","ideas":["Eels have no milt, spawn or genital passages and arise from earthworm-like growths in mud.","Fish gestation runs over periods divisible by seven days.","Shoal fishes spawn in a known order, from the atherine first to the cephalus last.","Fish of the Pontus grow very fast, such as the young tunny."],"terms":[{"t":"earth's guts (gēs entera)","d":"Worm-like growths in mud and damp ground, from which Aristotle says eels arise."}]},{"i":709,"j":717,"loc":"Book VI (6 of 9)","title":"Sexual passion and heat in viviparous animals","summary":"Aristotle notes that all animals are most excited by desire and the pleasure of copulation. Males grow fierce in the breeding season, as with stallions, wild boars that toughen their hides with mud, bulls, rams, camels and wild beasts, while females are fiercest after giving birth. He reports that Indian elephant-keepers keep males from females because they go wild. He describes the heat of mares and cows, the mare's wind-impregnation and the substance called hippomanes, and how stallions guard their mares and bulls leave the herd. He then discusses menstruation-like discharges in quadrupeds, the urine of various animals, and how females fatten in pregnancy.","ideas":["Males are most savage in the rutting season and females just after giving birth.","Frequent pairing in domestic animals lessens such frenzy.","The mare is the most lustful female and is said to be wind-impregnated.","Quadrupeds have smaller discharges than women, in proportion to their size."],"terms":[{"t":"hippomanes","d":"A substance from the mare in heat, or a growth on the new-born foal, sought by women who deal in drugs."}]},{"i":718,"j":726,"loc":"Book VI (7 of 9)","title":"Breeding of swine, sheep, goats and dogs","summary":"Aristotle gives breeding details for domestic animals. Sows carry for four months, bear up to twenty, and must be put to the boar only when they drop their ears. Ewes and she-goats carry for five months and may bear twice a year in good conditions; he describes the trained bell-wether and folk beliefs that north winds give male lambs and that lambs' colour follows the veins under the ram's tongue. He then treats dogs, chiefly the Laconian hound: age of breeding, gestation of sixty or more days, how long the pups are blind, heat, milk and litter size. He notes that critics praised Homer for making the dog of Ulysses die at twenty, and that dogs shed only their canine teeth.","ideas":["Gestation is four months in swine and five in sheep and goats.","Breeders believe winds, waters and the sire affect the sex and colour of lambs.","Laconian hounds breed at eight months, and males are hardier when hard-worked.","A dog's age is read from its teeth."],"terms":[{"t":"bell-wether","d":"A ram trained from youth to lead the flock when the shepherd calls it by name."}]},{"i":727,"j":746,"loc":"Book VI (8 of 9)","title":"Cattle, horses, asses and mules","summary":"Aristotle describes cattle: the bull impregnates at one mount, the cow carries nine or ten months, and the bull is strongest at five, which critics link to Homer's epithets. He gives a long account of horses: breeding from two or three years, an eleven-month gestation, how the horse sheds its teeth, how long horses live, and the mare's habit of biting off the hippomanes from her foal. He notes that an ass covering a pregnant mare destroys the embryo. He then treats the ass, crosses between horse and ass, whose foals resemble the mother, the fertility of mules and the dwarf hinny, and the old mule of Athens protected by public decree. He ends with a breeders' test of age by pinching the skin of the jaw.","ideas":["Horses are the most lustful animals after man, and mares live longer than stallions.","The horse's age is read from the shedding and wear of its teeth.","In crosses the gestation follows the male, while the foal favours the mother.","Mules rarely breed, though Syrophoenician she-mules are said to bear young."],"terms":[{"t":"hinny (ginnus)","d":"A stunted mule foaled by a mare that fell sick in pregnancy, likened to a human dwarf."},{"t":"gnomons","d":"The ass's fourth set of teeth, used to tell its age."}]},{"i":747,"j":768,"loc":"Book VI (9 of 9)","title":"Camel, elephant and wild beasts; prolific mice","summary":"Aristotle gives breeding facts for the camel and elephant, admitting that elephant gestation is uncertain because no one has seen them pair. He describes the wild sow, quoting Homer on the castrated boar, and the hind's breeding, her lair, and the stag's rut, dismissing fables about deer longevity. He treats the bear, whose cub is the smallest relative to its mother, and the lion, rejecting the fable that the lioness discharges her womb in giving birth. He corrects the belief that hyenas are both sexes, then covers hares, foxes, wolves (noting the Leto myth without endorsing it), cats, panthers and civets. He ends with the Syrian wild mule and the astonishing breeding of mice.","ideas":["Several popular stories, about the lioness, the hyena and the she-wolf, are rejected or left unverified.","The newborn bear cub is tiny and unformed, smaller than a weasel.","Hares superfoetate and bear young every month.","Field mice multiply beyond control and vanish only after heavy rain."],"terms":[{"t":"superfoetation","d":"Conceiving again while already pregnant, as hares do."}]},{"i":769,"j":793,"loc":"Book VII (1 of 3)","title":"Puberty, menstruation, conception and pregnancy in women","summary":"Book VII turns to man. Aristotle describes puberty at about fourteen: seed and pubic hair in boys, with Alcmaeon's comparison to plants blossoming before seeding; the breaking voice; and in girls the swelling breasts and onset of the catamenia. He urges that young people be watched at this age because early indulgence forms habits. He discusses changes of constitution, fertility after twenty-one, and menstruation, including the claim that the moon is feminine. He explains how the menstrual residue in other animals goes to hair, scales or feathers but in women is discharged, and gives signs of conception, means to aid or prevent it, and the early stages of the embryo. He ends with the discomforts and cravings of pregnancy and supposed differences between carrying boys and girls.","ideas":["Puberty in both sexes comes at about twice seven years, but fertility comes later.","In women the bodily residue is discharged as the catamenia; other animals use it for hair, scales or feathers.","Effluxion is loss of the embryo in the first week; abortion occurs up to the fortieth day.","Male embryos are said to move and differentiate sooner than female, though with many exceptions."],"terms":[{"t":"catamenia","d":"The monthly discharge in women, which after conception turns towards the breasts as milk."},{"t":"effluxion","d":"Loss of the conceived seed within the first seven days."}]},{"i":794,"j":814,"loc":"Book VII (2 of 3)","title":"Terms of pregnancy, multiple births and heredity","summary":"Aristotle notes that man alone has no fixed term of pregnancy, which may last seven to ten or even eleven months. Seven-month children can survive, while eight-month children seldom live in Greece, though they do in Egypt. He records births of twins, triplets and up to five children, the poor survival of mixed-sex twins, and cases of superfoetation, citing Iphicles and Hercules. He then discusses when fertility begins and ends, couples fertile only with other partners, and changes in the sex of offspring with age. Finally he treats inheritance: deformities and marks may pass to children or skip generations, as with the woman of Elis, and children may resemble either parent.","ideas":["Human pregnancy alone varies in length, from seven to eleven months.","Women may bear up to five at a birth, and superfoetation sometimes occurs.","Fertility in women usually ends at about forty to fifty, and in men at sixty to seventy.","Marks and traits may be inherited, sometimes reappearing after a generation."],"terms":[{"t":"superfoetation","d":"A second conception during an existing pregnancy, rare in women but recorded."}]},{"i":815,"j":833,"loc":"Book VII (3 of 3)","title":"Embryo in the womb, childbirth and early infancy","summary":"Aristotle describes how a membrane forms around the seed in the womb, and how all animals develop alike within membranes, nourished through the navel attached to the womb, cotyledon or egg. He describes the posture of embryos, their head-first birth, and the vessels of the navel-string. He then turns to labour: where the pains are felt, the waters, the afterbirth, and the midwife's careful tying of the cord, noting that skilled midwives have revived a seemingly dead infant by pressing blood back from the cord. He describes the newborn's first cry, the meconium, sleep and late ossification of the skull, teething, the flow of milk and breast ailments, and ends with infant convulsions and the custom of naming a child on the seventh day.","ideas":["All animals develop within membranes and grow through the navel.","Birth naturally takes place head foremost.","The nurse's skill in tying and cutting the navel-string can decide the child's life.","Most infant deaths happen in the first week, hence naming at seven days."],"terms":[{"t":"cotyledon","d":"A structure on the womb wall to which the navel is attached in some animals."},{"t":"meconium","d":"The newborn's first dark excrement, called 'poppy-juice' by midwives."}]},{"i":834,"j":845,"loc":"Book VIII (1 of 9)","title":"Animal character and the scale of nature","summary":"Aristotle turns from bodies and generation to habits and ways of life. He holds that animals show traces of psychical qualities such as gentleness, courage, or cunning, which in man differ by degree or by analogy, as the case of children shows. Nature passes by small steps from lifeless things to plants to animals, so that sea-creatures like the pinna, sponges and sea-anemones are hard to class. He then distinguishes three senses of 'terrestrial' and 'aquatic' (by breathing, by bodily temperament, by food), treats the dolphin and the cordylus as puzzling cases, and explains such differences by small changes in minute organs of the embryo, as in castration.","ideas":["Human and animal psychical traits are identical, similar, or analogous","No sharp line divides lifeless things, plants and animals","Animals are terrestrial or aquatic by respiration, temperament, or diet","Small changes in minute organs produce large changes of form"],"terms":[{"t":"scala naturae (scale of nature)","d":"The gradual ascent from lifeless things through plants to animals with no exact line between them."},{"t":"amphibious","d":"Applied to animals that breathe air and breed on land but feed and live mostly in water."}]},{"i":846,"j":856,"loc":"Book VIII (2 of 9)","title":"Food of shellfish, crustaceans, molluscs and fishes","summary":"Aristotle surveys what aquatic animals eat. Fixed testaceans live on fresh water strained out of the sea, which he proves with an empty wax vessel let down into the sea. Sea-anemones, limpets, murexes, sea-turtles, crawfish and octopuses are described in turn, including the crawfish–octopus–conger cycle of mastery. He then lists carnivorous, mud-eating and weed-eating fishes, singles out the mullet as not eating its own kind, and the parrot-wrasse as the one cud-chewing fish. The section closes on the care of eels, which need clear flowing water.","ideas":["Fresh water is contained in sea water and can be filtered out","The crawfish fears the octopus, masters the conger, and the conger eats the octopus","Most fishes eat their own kind; mullets eat no flesh","Eels die in muddy or unchanged water because their gills are small"],"terms":[{"t":"testaceans","d":"Shelled animals such as oysters and murexes, some fixed in place like plants."}]},{"i":857,"j":867,"loc":"Book VIII (3 of 9)","title":"What birds and scaly reptiles eat","summary":"Birds with crooked talons are all carnivorous and cannot swallow grain; Aristotle lists eagles, hawks, vultures and night-owls with their sizes. Other birds feed on grubs, thistles, insects under bark, or fruit and herbage, and he describes the golden-crested wren, woodpeckers and the pigeons, noting which are seen all year. He divides birds by feeding ground into land, river-and-lake, and sea birds, cataloguing herons, kingfishers, swans, cormorants and others. Birds of prey rarely drink. Tessellated reptiles are omnivorous; serpents are great gluttons, fond of wine, and swallow prey whole.","ideas":["Crooked-taloned birds are strictly carnivorous","Birds divide into land, freshwater and sea feeders","Birds of prey hardly ever drink","Serpents are caught with saucers of wine"],"terms":[{"t":"tessellates (pholidota)","d":"Animals covered with horny scales, such as lizards and serpents."}]},{"i":868,"j":883,"loc":"Book VIII (4 of 9)","title":"Diets of quadrupeds, fattening and insects","summary":"Jag-toothed quadrupeds are carnivorous, eating grass only to purge themselves. Aristotle describes the hyaena, the bear's method of downing a bull, the lion's feeding, and river animals such as the beaver and otter, then explains lapping, sucking and gulping as ways of drinking. Most of the section is practical husbandry: fattening pigs after starvation, fattening cattle and the Pyrrhic kine of Epirus, horses fattened by drink, the elephant's and camel's capacities, and salting sheep. It ends with insects, noting that the bee alone shuns anything rotten.","ideas":["Carnivores eat grass only when sick","Animals fatten best after a short course of starvation","Water quality governs the feeding of beasts of burden","The bee touches only sweet food"],"terms":[{"t":"jag-toothed (karcharodont)","d":"Having interlocking saw-like teeth, the mark of fierce carnivorous quadrupeds."}]},{"i":884,"j":898,"loc":"Book VIII (5 of 9)","title":"Seasonal migrations of birds and fishes","summary":"All habits concern breeding or food and are adjusted to heat, cold and season, so animals shift their haunts as rich men move between summer and winter homes. Cranes fly from Scythia to the marshes above Egypt, where Aristotle says they really fight a race of pygmies; pelicans, quails, doves and others migrate, and the tale of the crane's touchstone is rejected. Fishes migrate between the outer sea and the Euxine for food and for spawning in its fresh water. He records shore and deep-sea fishes, the trichiae's supposed route through the Danube to the Adriatic, and the tunny keeping the shore on its right.","ideas":["Weaker animals migrate earlier in the face of heat or cold","The pygmies who fight cranes are a real people","Fish enter the Euxine for richer food and fresh spawning water","Shore fish taste better than deep-sea fish"],"terms":[{"t":"Euxine","d":"The Black Sea, entered by shoal fish in summer."}]},{"i":899,"j":915,"loc":"Book VIII (6 of 9)","title":"Hibernation, torpor and the sloughing of skins","summary":"Aristotle describes animals that hide in winter or summer: shellfish, insects including the bee, reptiles, many fishes such as the tunny, and birds like the swallow, kite and turtle-dove, which he says often hide rather than migrate. The bear's winter retreat is treated at length: it grows fat, gives birth in hiding, eats nothing, and on emerging eats arum to open its gut. He then turns to sloughing the 'old-age', the outer skin: serpents peel from the eyes to the tail, insects shed husks like an afterbirth, and crawfish, lobsters and crabs moult repeatedly.","ideas":["Many birds hide in torpor instead of migrating","The she-bear gives birth during winter concealment","Serpents slough from head to tail in a day and a night","Insects break from their husk as young from an afterbirth"],"terms":[{"t":"gēras ('old-age')","d":"The name for the outer skin or casing sloughed off by snakes, insects and crustaceans."}]},{"i":916,"j":930,"loc":"Book VIII (7 of 9)","title":"Weather, health and disease in birds, fishes and shellfish","summary":"Animals thrive at different seasons: birds in drought, most fishes in rain, which Aristotle supports by the Euxine's fresh river-fed waters; he notes Hesiod's error in making an eagle drink. Some fishes suffer in cold or rain, such as those with a stone in the head and the cephalus, which goes blind. He describes the parasite that drives the sword-fish to leap, why fishes are caught at dawn and dusk, and peculiar ailments of river fish. Methods of catching fish with mullein, trenches and weirs follow, and the section ends with the effects of rain and drought on shellfish.","ideas":["Rain benefits most fishes, drought most birds","Fishes have no pestilences but suffer particular sicknesses","Fish are most easily caught at sunrise and sunset","Drought and dredging destroyed the clams of the Pyrrhaean Strait"],"terms":[]},{"i":931,"j":943,"loc":"Book VIII (8 of 9)","title":"Diseases of quadrupeds and insects","summary":"Aristotle catalogues the diseases of domestic animals and their remedies. Pigs suffer branchos, craurus and measles, the last unique to pigs; dogs suffer rabies, which infects every animal but man, quinsy and sore feet. Cattle, pastured horses and stall-fed horses each have their ailments, with symptoms such as the horse's nymphia at flute music; he dismisses the tales about hippomanes as old wives' stories. The ass's melis and the elephant's complaints and cures follow. He ends with pests of bee-hives and the fact that oil kills all insects.","ideas":["The pig alone suffers measles","Rabies passes to all animals bitten except man","Stall-fed horses have many more diseases than pastured ones","Stories about hippomanes come from charm-sellers"],"terms":[{"t":"hippomanes","d":"A growth on the newborn foal that the mare licks off, the subject of magical tales Aristotle rejects."}]},{"i":944,"j":955,"loc":"Book VIII (9 of 9)","title":"Effects of locality and breeding on animals","summary":"Place shapes animal life: cicadas, weasels and moles appear on one side of a river or road and not the other, and hares die on Ithaca. Aristotle compares sizes in Egypt and Greece, tracing differences to food and climate, and repeats the saying that Libya always has something new, where species are said to cross at watering-places. He reports hybrid dogs and notes how locality alters temperament and the danger of scorpion and serpent bites. He then shows how pregnancy and spawning affect the condition and colour of shellfish, molluscs and fishes for the table.","ideas":["Neighbouring districts may differ sharply in their fauna","Wild animals are wildest in Asia, boldest in Europe, most varied in Libya","Bites are more or less deadly by region","Fishes change colour and quality with the breeding season"],"terms":[]},{"i":956,"j":976,"loc":"Book IX (1 of 10)","title":"Character, the sexes and animal enmities","summary":"Book IX opens on the dispositions of longer-lived animals and their capacity to learn. Aristotle sets out the contrast between female and male character, claiming it is plainest in man, and lists traits he ascribes to woman and man. Enmity arises among animals sharing a locality or food, and he argues that with ample food animals would be tame, citing Egypt's tame crocodiles. A long catalogue of hostile and friendly pairs of birds, beasts and fishes follows, with an account of how Indians capture and tame elephants. He concludes that friendship and enmity depend on food and way of life.","ideas":["Females are softer and more cunning, males more spirited, except bears and leopards","Scarcity of food is the source of animal hostility","Shoaling fish are friendly, solitary ones hostile","Wild elephants are tamed by tame ones"],"terms":[{"t":"êthos (character, disposition)","d":"The temperament of an animal, such as courage, timidity, or cunning."}]},{"i":977,"j":995,"loc":"Book IX (2 of 10)","title":"Intelligence and remedies of quadrupeds","summary":"Aristotle compares quadrupeds for stupidity and intelligence: the sheep is the most foolish, while herd habits of goats, cattle and mares are described. The hind is the most intelligent wild quadruped, giving birth beside roads and hiding its shed horns; the yearly growth of antlers and the hunting of deer with music follow. Many animals treat themselves: Cretan goats seek dittany, dogs eat grass, the panther seeks human excrement, the tortoise eats marjoram after a snake. He adds the ichneumon's mud armour, the trochilus cleaning the crocodile's teeth, and hedgehogs foretelling changes of wind.","ideas":["The sheep is the dullest of quadrupeds","Antlers grow more branched each year until the sixth","Animals use plants as remedies for wounds and poison","Crocodile and trochilus help each other"],"terms":[]},{"i":996,"j":1015,"loc":"Book IX (3 of 10)","title":"Birds' intelligence, nests and family life","summary":"Aristotle finds the closest likeness to human life in small creatures, beginning with the swallow, which builds of mud and chaff and feeds its young fairly. Pigeons keep one mate for life and the male helps at hatching; their lifespans are recorded. Ground-nesting quails and partridges lure hunters away by feigning lameness, and the partridge's quarrelsome mating and decoy-hunting are described. The woodpecker, cranes with their leader and sentinels, pelicans, hawks, the vulture with its unseen nest, the wren, and swans singing before death follow, with Homer's line on the cymindis.","ideas":["Small birds show the most striking intelligence","Pigeons keep a single mate until death","The partridge feigns lameness to protect its brood","Swans sing chiefly when about to die"],"terms":[]},{"i":1016,"j":1038,"loc":"Book IX (4 of 10)","title":"Catalogue of birds, from stork to raven","summary":"Aristotle runs through many birds by nest, eggs, plumage and temper. He reports that young storks and bee-eaters feed their parents, describes the penduline tit's woven nest, the reported cinnamon bird, and the halcyon's sponge-like nest, perhaps made of fish bones. Short notes follow on the hoopoe, herons, owsels, thrushes, daws, larks, ibises and little owls. The cuckoo lays its egg in another bird's nest from awareness of its own cowardice, and the young cuckoo's foster brood perishes. The goat-sucker and the raven, which pairs in small districts, close the section.","ideas":["The cuckoo makes its young a supposititious child in an alien nest","The halcyon builds a nest that resists the sea","Ravens seem able to communicate across distances","Most wild birds change appearance between summer and winter"],"terms":[]},{"i":1039,"j":1049,"loc":"Book IX (5 of 10)","title":"Eagles, hawks and hunting partnerships","summary":"Aristotle lists the species of eagle, praising the small black eagle and faulting the percnopterus, and describes the 'true-bred' eagle as the only unmixed kind. Eagles hunt from midday, drive their young from wide territories, descend on hares gradually, and are said to be the only bird resembling the gods for their high flight. The phene rears young the eagle casts out, and the sea-eagle makes its young stare at the sun. He then ranks hawks and reports how pigeons distinguish them, ending with Thracian fowlers who share their catch with hawks and wolves that cooperate with fishermen at Lake Maeotis.","ideas":["A pair of eagles needs a large hunting territory","The sea-eagle tests its young by making them face the sun","Pigeons respond differently to different kinds of hawk","Hawks and wolves hunt in partnership with men"],"terms":[]},{"i":1050,"j":1058,"loc":"Book IX (6 of 10)","title":"Cunning devices of sea creatures","summary":"Aristotle confirms the stories of the fishing-frog, which lures prey with filaments, and the torpedo, which numbs its prey and even men. He describes the anthias as a sign of safe waters for sponge-divers, the sea-scolopendra turning itself inside out to eject a hook, bonitos defending their shoal, and the male sheat-fish guarding its spawn for forty or fifty days. Notes on the Pyrrha lagoon and fish breeding follow. The sepia uses its ink for concealment, while the octopus changes colour but is stupid and dies within a year; the nautilus sails with its webbed arms.","ideas":["Ambushing fish angle with filaments like fishing-rods","The torpedo numbs its prey","The male sheat-fish guards the young","The nautilus uses its web as a sail and feelers as oars"],"terms":[{"t":"torpedo (narkē)","d":"The electric ray, which numbs creatures by touch."}]},{"i":1059,"j":1074,"loc":"Book IX (7 of 10)","title":"Spiders and the life of the hive","summary":"The most industrious creatures are the ant, bee, hornet and wasp. Aristotle describes venomous spiders and the skilled web-weaver that lays warp and woof from an exact centre, rejecting Democritus' view that the thread is an excretion. He then lists nine kinds of honeycomb-building insects and begins a long account of bees: cell-building, stop-wax and mitys, gathering from one kind of flower per trip, the kings and drones, robber-bees, and the killing of surplus kings. He describes swarming, the division of labour, bees' enemies, and their death after stinging.","ideas":["The orb-weaver builds its web from a centre with warp and woof","Bees visit one kind of flower on each trip","Workers kill excess kings to keep the hive whole","A bee that loses its sting must die"],"terms":[{"t":"king-bee","d":"The leader of the hive, which leaves it only with a swarm."},{"t":"mitys","d":"A black, pungent by-product of wax smeared at the hive entrance."}]},{"i":1075,"j":1088,"loc":"Book IX (8 of 10)","title":"Beekeeping, wasps, hornets and humble-bees","summary":"Aristotle finishes the bees: the toad as predator, young bees that do outdoor work and do not sting, hive diseases, the best honey and the flowers bees use, and the waking and sleeping signals by single bees. He gives keepers practical advice on yields, trapping wasps, and plants to set by the hive. Wild and tame wasps are then described, with mother-wasps that overwinter and workers that die, nest-building from small beginnings, and uncertainty about sex and stings. Anthrenae (hornets) hunt flies and have one king per nest, and humble-bees and tenthredons close the account.","ideas":["Spring honey is better than autumn honey","One bee wakes the hive and one signals sleep","Wasp workers die in winter, mothers survive","Several questions about wasps and hornets remain unobserved"],"terms":[{"t":"anthrēnē (hornet)","d":"A stinging insect that lives on flesh and builds large underground nests."}]},{"i":1089,"j":1095,"loc":"Book IX (9 of 10)","title":"Lion, civet, bison, elephant and camel","summary":"Aristotle returns to the temper of wild animals. The lion is gentle when fed, retreats slowly in view of hunters, fears fire as Homer says, and attacks men only when old and toothless; two kinds are distinguished. The civet is friendly to man, and the bison of Paeonia is described in detail, including its defence by spraying caustic dung. The elephant is the gentlest and most teachable wild animal, with reports of its lifespan. Stories of a camel and a Scythian horse that refused to mate with their mothers end the section.","ideas":["The lion attacks men chiefly in old age","The bison defends itself with blistering dung","The elephant is the easiest wild animal to tame","Camels and horses are said to shun mating with their mothers"],"terms":[{"t":"bonasos (bison)","d":"A maned wild ox of Mount Messapium, called monapos by the Paeonians."}]},{"i":1096,"j":1107,"loc":"Book IX (10 of 10)","title":"Dolphins, castration and seasonal changes","summary":"Aristotle reports stories of the dolphin's gentleness, care for its kind and great speed, by which it can leap ship's masts. He then argues that a change of action brings a change of character and even body, as with hens that beat a cock and crow, and turns to castration in birds, men, cattle, sows and camels and its effects on hair, voice, size and horns. Rumination in animals lacking upper teeth follows. He closes on birds that change colour and song by season, quoting Aeschylus' lines on the hoopoe, and on dust-bathing and water-bathing birds.","ideas":["Dolphins aid captured or dead companions","Castration changes form, voice and growth depending on age","Some birds thought to be two species are one bird in different seasons","Ground birds take dust-baths, waterside birds water-baths"],"terms":[{"t":"rumination","d":"Chewing the cud, practised by animals lacking upper teeth, such as cattle, sheep and goats."}]}]});